WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.380
Instructor: So now that we know that Git

00:01.380 --> 00:04.830
is a version control system, a popular one at that,

00:04.830 --> 00:06.870
let's talk more about the specific features.

00:06.870 --> 00:08.250
What does it do for us?

00:08.250 --> 00:09.870
So in this slide, we can see some

00:09.870 --> 00:11.760
of the main things Git helps us with.

00:11.760 --> 00:15.120
It helps track changes across files, compare versions

00:15.120 --> 00:18.300
of a project, time travel back to old versions,

00:18.300 --> 00:20.940
take a peek at what something looks like a year ago.

00:20.940 --> 00:23.130
Take a look at Facebook's code base today

00:23.130 --> 00:25.920
compared to two days ago,

00:25.920 --> 00:27.480
go back to a previous version

00:27.480 --> 00:29.550
if we mess something up,

00:29.550 --> 00:32.340
combine different changes between versions,

00:32.340 --> 00:35.100
between different files, between different collaborators,

00:35.100 --> 00:37.020
and that's probably the most important part,

00:37.020 --> 00:39.120
collaborate and share changes.

00:39.120 --> 00:40.890
Git facilitates collaboration

00:40.890 --> 00:44.040
between a team of users, of developers, of writers,

00:44.040 --> 00:46.080
whatever people are doing with Git.

00:46.080 --> 00:48.030
Git facilitates collaboration

00:48.030 --> 00:51.930
and sharing those changes between individual people.

00:51.930 --> 00:54.753
So here's a very, very simple explanation.

00:55.590 --> 00:58.950
If you've ever played video games, like I have,

00:58.950 --> 01:01.770
there are some games that allow you to make save points.

01:01.770 --> 01:04.230
Little checkpoints you can come back to,

01:04.230 --> 01:06.963
and I guess this is supposed to be me.

01:08.340 --> 01:11.730
I was always very, very nervous as a game player.

01:11.730 --> 01:13.980
Some of the games I played as a kid where if you died,

01:13.980 --> 01:15.060
you would go all the way back

01:15.060 --> 01:16.890
to wherever your last save was.

01:16.890 --> 01:19.770
So I would always try and save before, well,

01:19.770 --> 01:21.690
pretty much before anything.

01:21.690 --> 01:24.750
So I would save and then get to some horrible boss battle.

01:24.750 --> 01:27.060
I suck at video games, even though I like them.

01:27.060 --> 01:27.900
I die.

01:27.900 --> 01:29.190
But that's okay.

01:29.190 --> 01:30.240
I don't get angry.

01:30.240 --> 01:31.470
Because I saved my game,

01:31.470 --> 01:33.390
I can just go back to that save point.

01:33.390 --> 01:37.620
That is the simplest, simplest explanation of Git

01:37.620 --> 01:40.830
and what it allows us to do, set up these checkpoints.

01:40.830 --> 01:42.390
You can screw things up entirely,

01:42.390 --> 01:45.270
make a huge mess of a code base or of a project.

01:45.270 --> 01:47.910
Empty all the files out, delete the files.

01:47.910 --> 01:50.700
But if you're using Git and you made these checkpoints,

01:50.700 --> 01:51.960
we can just go back.

01:51.960 --> 01:54.780
So here's another explanation that I've used.

01:54.780 --> 01:57.330
In high school and college, I had to write a lot of essays.

01:57.330 --> 01:59.430
I did not know Git really at the time.

01:59.430 --> 02:00.630
So I did this.

02:00.630 --> 02:02.730
I would start writing an essay

02:02.730 --> 02:04.590
and then I wanted to try something new.

02:04.590 --> 02:08.100
So I'd Save As, I'd make a new version that was v1

02:08.100 --> 02:11.400
or v2, or even more variations.

02:11.400 --> 02:15.540
V2 with a new intro or a new conclusion or reorder,

02:15.540 --> 02:17.190
all sorts of different strategies.

02:17.190 --> 02:18.870
And I would save all of them

02:18.870 --> 02:20.220
because I wanted to be able to compare.

02:20.220 --> 02:23.130
I didn't wanna just have one essay

02:23.130 --> 02:24.570
that I had to work in, right?

02:24.570 --> 02:27.120
I wanna be able to track versions and compare

02:27.120 --> 02:29.550
and it's just part of the process.

02:29.550 --> 02:31.560
And then I would end up with some final,

02:31.560 --> 02:34.410
but then inevitably, actually final, final for real.

02:34.410 --> 02:36.750
This is the one, print this one.

02:36.750 --> 02:38.850
This was honestly what I did in high school

02:38.850 --> 02:40.470
and college all the time.

02:40.470 --> 02:42.810
So Git helps with this sort of issue.

02:42.810 --> 02:46.170
Now, this is a very trivial situation, but imagine thousands

02:46.170 --> 02:48.270
of developers collaborating on a project

02:48.270 --> 02:50.910
where they're working on millions of lines of code

02:50.910 --> 02:53.430
all at the same time and having these different versions

02:53.430 --> 02:55.620
and sharing and combining them.

02:55.620 --> 02:58.650
So here's a somewhat more realistic example.

02:58.650 --> 03:01.140
I'm working on some project, a website.

03:01.140 --> 03:04.380
This is the progress of the website up top.

03:04.380 --> 03:05.730
So right now, it's empty.

03:05.730 --> 03:08.790
With Git, I create checkpoints.

03:08.790 --> 03:12.180
So checkpoints are like save files I can go back to.

03:12.180 --> 03:15.180
So I have my first checkpoint, Initialized Project.

03:15.180 --> 03:16.410
There's nothing up there.

03:16.410 --> 03:17.520
But then I do some work.

03:17.520 --> 03:19.470
I add this navbar up top.

03:19.470 --> 03:22.680
So I make a new checkpoint, Add Top Navbar.

03:22.680 --> 03:25.500
Now I have two checkpoints, and then I do more work, right?

03:25.500 --> 03:26.910
Some content.

03:26.910 --> 03:28.770
So I add a new checkpoint.

03:28.770 --> 03:30.810
We'll say Add First Row, whatever this is.

03:30.810 --> 03:32.760
Maybe I'm making YouTube or something.

03:32.760 --> 03:35.310
So I add some content and then I do more work.

03:35.310 --> 03:37.950
I add more content, I add a new checkpoint.

03:37.950 --> 03:39.930
This workflow is very, very common.

03:39.930 --> 03:41.250
You do something in a project.

03:41.250 --> 03:42.270
If you're using Git,

03:42.270 --> 03:44.370
you make one of these so-called checkpoints.

03:44.370 --> 03:46.890
We'll learn what they are and how you do it, of course.

03:46.890 --> 03:48.210
And then you keep working.

03:48.210 --> 03:50.100
So here, I change the theme entirely.

03:50.100 --> 03:53.880
A dark theme, new fonts, new colors, new everything.

03:53.880 --> 03:55.500
I add a checkpoint.

03:55.500 --> 03:58.170
So I forgot to mention, I work not alone on this.

03:58.170 --> 03:59.280
I'm working at a company.

03:59.280 --> 04:00.240
I have a boss.

04:00.240 --> 04:02.790
I'm not the one making these decisions, right?

04:02.790 --> 04:05.130
What stays, if we want this theme or not,

04:05.130 --> 04:07.110
or if we want this earlier theme here.

04:07.110 --> 04:09.240
But anyway, I do some more work.

04:09.240 --> 04:12.060
I alter the navbar color, I make it yellow.

04:12.060 --> 04:14.610
I add a checkpoint, update navbar,

04:14.610 --> 04:17.070
and then I show it to my boss.

04:17.070 --> 04:19.350
Realistically, you don't just show code to your boss,

04:19.350 --> 04:22.470
but there's a process for approving or rejecting changes.

04:22.470 --> 04:25.950
But anyway, in this simplistic world, my boss gets angry,

04:25.950 --> 04:27.990
or maybe not even angry, she just says, you know what?

04:27.990 --> 04:28.890
These colors suck.

04:28.890 --> 04:32.070
We can't move forward with that, but that's okay.

04:32.070 --> 04:32.903
I use Git.

04:32.903 --> 04:34.380
I have this series of checkpoints.

04:34.380 --> 04:37.170
I can go back in time to earlier checkpoints.

04:37.170 --> 04:39.660
So this is what it looked like at this checkpoint,

04:39.660 --> 04:41.820
but I can go backwards too.

04:41.820 --> 04:44.730
So from before the time I added the dark theme.

04:44.730 --> 04:46.680
Now we're back to this light theme.

04:46.680 --> 04:48.600
And then I could even branch off.

04:48.600 --> 04:52.470
I could start working from this point and create a new sort

04:52.470 --> 04:56.160
of fork where I can have an alternate timeline.

04:56.160 --> 04:59.340
So this work's not lost, I'm just not doing it right now.

04:59.340 --> 05:01.470
I'm working on a new version.

05:01.470 --> 05:04.263
So I added more content, if you can see that there.

05:05.100 --> 05:06.810
So I had two rows of content.

05:06.810 --> 05:07.800
I add more content.

05:07.800 --> 05:10.980
Now, I've picked up development at this point in time.

05:10.980 --> 05:15.480
So I add a third row, maybe I add a new footer down there.

05:15.480 --> 05:16.800
So we get a new checkpoint.

05:16.800 --> 05:19.710
If I want to add a checkpoint then, and then,

05:19.710 --> 05:22.050
I could switch back to what I had earlier.

05:22.050 --> 05:24.750
At this point, I can toggle between them.

05:24.750 --> 05:27.810
I can use Git to say go all the way back to when I first

05:27.810 --> 05:30.090
added the navbar or go all the way up to

05:30.090 --> 05:31.770
when I updated the navbar.

05:31.770 --> 05:34.170
I made it yellow or go to Add Footer.

05:34.170 --> 05:36.930
I can hop around and compare and I could present

05:36.930 --> 05:38.100
both to my boss.

05:38.100 --> 05:41.220
They both can exist, which is the crazy part.

05:41.220 --> 05:44.550
Git helps us manage all of these different versions.

05:44.550 --> 05:47.070
So I can switch back to this dark mode checkpoint,

05:47.070 --> 05:49.950
but what's even crazier, I can even combine

05:49.950 --> 05:50.820
these checkpoints,

05:50.820 --> 05:53.100
the work that changes between the two.

05:53.100 --> 05:56.970
So this dark mode had, you know, the dark colors

05:56.970 --> 05:59.460
And Add Footer had all this extra content,

05:59.460 --> 06:01.170
with a footer and a third row.

06:01.170 --> 06:04.320
So I combined them to end up with a footer

06:04.320 --> 06:08.130
and a third row of content and the dark mode colors.

06:08.130 --> 06:09.630
So I now have a hybrid,

06:09.630 --> 06:12.390
and that's exactly the sort of thing that Git helps us with.

06:12.390 --> 06:14.430
Now, this is a simplified example,

06:14.430 --> 06:16.470
but imagine now I'm working on a project.

06:16.470 --> 06:17.550
It's not just me.

06:17.550 --> 06:20.070
There's 10 people or a hundred developers,

06:20.070 --> 06:22.110
lots of files, lots of versions.

06:22.110 --> 06:23.940
How do we coordinate all those changes

06:23.940 --> 06:26.040
between all of us and combine them, right?

06:26.040 --> 06:28.440
We don't all just work on in silos on our own.

06:28.440 --> 06:31.080
We need to combine that work back into some code base

06:31.080 --> 06:33.540
at some point, or whatever we're working on,

06:33.540 --> 06:35.130
we share those changes.

06:35.130 --> 06:38.010
So it's this times hundreds and hundreds,

06:38.010 --> 06:39.990
often at a large company.

06:39.990 --> 06:41.580
Okay, so that's the basic idea

06:41.580 --> 06:43.350
of Git and what it can help us with.

06:43.350 --> 06:45.210
Just trying to visualize it for you.

06:45.210 --> 06:47.280
Next up, I guess, totally optional.

06:47.280 --> 06:49.280
I'm gonna talk about the history of Git.
